<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="flavonoid biosynthesis (in equisetum)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6787" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: The expected taxonomic range of the pathway includes Oryza sativa Japonica Group, and, enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Flavonoids are secondary metabolites formed from phenylpropanoid and fatty acid derivatives and have important functions, acting as UV-B protectors |CITS:[11724379]|, attractors of pollinators and seed dispersers, insect feeding attractors, anti-feedants, signal molecules in legume-rhizobium bacteria interactions |CITS:[3738520]|, and fungicides. Flavonoids have also received a lot of attention due to their effect of human health. In some plant species, flavonoids are required for fertility, but this is not the case in |FRAME:TAX-3701| |CITS:[8672888]|. Biosynthesis is thought to occur in the cytoplasm through enzymes that are associated with the endoplasmatic reticulum membranes |CITS:[3321063]| where the enzymes form a large, macromolecular complex |CITS:[10536025]|. The structure of the major flavonoids in |FRAME:TAX-3701| have recently been described |CITS:[10514319]|. Under greenhouse conditions, only kaempferol glycosides accumulate in |FRAME:TAX-3701|. With higher UV exposure, quercetin metabolites can be detected as well |CITS:[10514319]|. Most flavonoids accumulate in the vacuole, the cuticular wax and in the cell wall |CITS:[10975502]|. Flavonoids are secondary metabolites formed from phenylpropanoid and fatty acid derivatives and have important functions, acting as UV-B protectors |CITS:[11724379]|, attractors of pollinators and seed dispersers, insect feeding attractors, anti-feedants, signal molecules in legume-rhizobium bacteria interactions |CITS:[3738520]|, and fungicides. Flavonoids have also received a lot of attention due to their effect of human health. In some plant species, flavonoids are required for fertility, but this is not the case in |FRAME:TAX-3701| |CITS:[8672888]|. Biosynthesis is thought to occur in the cytoplasm through enzymes that are associated with the endoplasmic reticulum membranes |CITS:[3321063]| where the enzymes form a large, macromolecular complex |CITS:[10536025]|. The structure of the major flavonoids in |FRAME:TAX-3701| have recently been described |CITS:[10514319]|. Under greenhouse conditions, only kaempferol glycosides accumulate in |FRAME:TAX-3701|. With higher UV exposure, quercetin metabolites can be detected as well |CITS:[10514319]|. Most flavonoids accumulate in the vacuole, the cuticular wax and in the cell wall |CITS:[10975502]|. Flavonoids constitute a relatively diverse family of aromatic molecules that are derived from Phenylalanine and malonyl-coenzyme A (CoA; via the fatty acidic pathway). |CITS: [11402179]| There are six major subgroups found in most higher plants: the chalcones, flavones, flavonols, flavandiols, anthocyanins, and condensed tannins (proanthocyanidins); a seventh group, the aurones, are not ubiquitos. Isoflavonoids are found mostly in legumes but also in a small number of non-legume plants and another specialized form of flavonoids, the phlobaphenes (monomers: 3-deoxyanthocyanins), have been detected in only a few species so far. Stilbenes, closely related to flavonoids, are synthesized by yet another group of unrelated species. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: TAX-3258| (horsetail) grows in moist areas of temperate regions. The shoot has high levels of silicate and has been traditionally used as a cleansing agent. It is also used for a wide range of medicinal uses as it has a number of secondary metabolites that are bioactive agents|CITS: [21301385]|. The infertile shoots posses high levels of flavonoids, caffeic esters and complex glucosides. The fertile portion or the sporophyte lacks flavonoids. The equisetum flavonoids were shown to have antioxidant, germination-inhibitory and hepatoprotective effects |CITS: [20170379]|, |CITS: [20011829]|. The protoflavones; protoapigenin and protogenkwaninare are promising anticarcinogenic agents. This pathway shows the flavonoid biosynthesis in infertile shoots of horsetail. 2-oxoglutarate-dependent dioxygenases convert flavonones, like NAR or eriodictyol, to flavones, dihydroflavonols and flavonols. The expression of flavone synthase I was demonstrated for the first time to occur in a plant taxon outside of Apiaceae |CITS: [21353683]|.</Description>
